---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import ArchitectureDiagram from '../components/ArchitectureDiagram.astro';
import { platforms, totals } from '../data/platforms';

const handlerCode = `service.get('/orders', (req, res) => {
  // The gateway already resolved the tenant, applied
  // its package ACL to this API and checked the user.
  const { tenant, urac, inputmaskData } = req.soajs;

  orders.list(tenant.id, urac._id, inputmaskData, (err, data) =>
    res.json(req.soajs.buildResponse(err, data)));
});`;

const serviceConfig = `module.exports = {
  type: 'service',
  serviceName: 'orders',
  serviceGroup: 'commerce',
  servicePort: 4100,
  requestTimeout: 30,
  extKeyRequired: true, // tenant key + package ACL
  oauth: true,          // access token required
  urac: true,           // user context on req.soajs
  maintenance: { readiness: '/heartbeat', port: { type: 'maintenance' } },
  schema: {
    get: {
      '/orders': {
        _apiInfo: { l: 'List orders', group: 'Orders' },
        limit: {
          source: ['query.limit'],
          default: 50,
          validation: { type: 'integer' }
        }
      }
    }
  }
};`;
---

<Base
	title="SOAJS"
	description="SOAJS is an open-source multi-tenant API gateway. Tenant keys, per-API ACL and OAuth work together at the gateway, so every customer gets exactly the access in their package and your microservices only contain business logic."
>
	<section class="hero">
		<div class="wrap hero-grid">
			<div>
			<p class="eyebrow">Open-source multi-tenant API gateway</p>
			<h1>Turn your microservices<br /><span>into multi-tenant products.</span></h1>
			<p class="lead">
				Before your code runs, the SOAJS gateway knows which customer is calling, what they are allowed to use and
				who the user is. Tenant keys, per-API ACL and OAuth work together, so each customer gets exactly the access
				in their package. Rate limiting, caching and idempotency come with it. You write the business logic.
			</p>
			<div class="actions">
				<a class="btn btn-primary" href="/docs/getting-started/quickstart/">Get started</a>
				<a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#how-it-fits">See how it works</a>
				<a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://github.com/soajs/soajs">Star on GitHub</a>
			</div>
			<div class="hero-meta">
				<span>Free and open source, Apache-2.0</span>
				<span>Node.js, Go and Python</span>
				<span>Cloud, on-prem or air-gapped</span>
			</div>
			</div>
			<div class="hero-code">
				<div class="code-label">orders/index.js: your whole handler</div>
				<Code code={handlerCode} lang="js" theme="github-dark" class="code" />
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="soft">
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="section-head">
				<p class="eyebrow">Sound familiar?</p>
				<h2>The plumbing every multi-tenant product ends up rebuilding</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="grid grid-2">
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Every customer needs different access</h3>
					<p class="muted">Plans, partners and enterprise deals turn into per-customer flags and special cases in business logic, one <code>if</code> at a time.</p>
					<p><strong>With SOAJS:</strong> each customer's key maps to a product package, and its ACL decides, API by API, what that customer can call.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Every service re-implements auth</h3>
					<p class="muted">Copy-pasted token checks drift across teams and languages until nobody is sure which rules apply where.</p>
					<p><strong>With SOAJS:</strong> OAuth tokens, tenant keys and user groups are checked once, at the gateway, for every service.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Traffic rules live in ten places</h3>
					<p class="muted">Rate limits, response caches and safe retries get rebuilt in each service, or skipped.</p>
					<p><strong>With SOAJS:</strong> rate limiting, GET caching and Idempotency-Key replay protection are gateway settings, per service.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Sockets and jobs skip the rules</h3>
					<p class="muted">Real-time servers and background workers grow a second, weaker copy of your access control.</p>
					<p><strong>With SOAJS:</strong> they authenticate through the same gateway and get the same ACL as HTTP calls.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>


	<section>
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="section-head">
				<p class="eyebrow">Tenants × ACL × OAuth</p>
				<h2>One set of services. Every customer gets exactly what they bought.</h2>
				<p class="lead">
					The power is in the combination. The key says which customer is calling, their package's ACL says what they
					can reach, and the OAuth token says which user is acting. The gateway applies all three on every request.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="grid grid-2" style="align-items:start">
				<div>
					<div class="table-wrap">
						<table class="acl">
							<caption>Example: one <em>Commerce</em> product, three packages, the same three APIs</caption>
							<thead>
								<tr><th scope="col">Caller</th><th scope="col"><code>GET /catalog</code></th><th scope="col"><code>POST /orders</code></th><th scope="col"><code>GET /reports</code></th></tr>
							</thead>
							<tbody>
								<tr><th scope="row">Acme<span>Pro package</span></th><td class="yes">Public</td><td class="yes">Signed-in users</td><td class="yes">Managers group only</td></tr>
								<tr><th scope="row">Globex<span>Basic package</span></th><td class="yes">Public</td><td class="yes">Signed-in users</td><td class="no">Not in package</td></tr>
								<tr><th scope="row">Storefront<span>Guest package</span></th><td class="yes">Public</td><td class="no">Not in package</td><td class="no">Not in package</td></tr>
							</tbody>
						</table>
					</div>
					<p class="muted" style="margin-top:12px;font-size:0.92rem">Same service, same code. Only the package each key belongs to is different.</p>
				</div>
				<ul class="checks">
					<li><strong>The key picks the tenant and the package.</strong> Each customer application gets its own external keys, with their own expiry.</li>
					<li><strong>The package ACL decides each API.</strong> Public, signed-in only, or limited to user groups, per environment, service version and HTTP method.</li>
					<li><strong>The OAuth user can widen or narrow access.</strong> URAC groups can grant their own packages, so an admin inside a tenant sees more than a regular user.</li>
					<li><strong>Change access without touching services.</strong> Update a package in the console and the gateway enforces it for every service behind it.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="soft">
		<div class="wrap grid grid-2" style="align-items:start">
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow">What you stop writing</p>
				<h2>Every check runs before your code does</h2>
				<p class="lead">
					Every call goes through the same ordered pipeline. Set <code>extKeyRequired</code>, <code>oauth</code> and
					<code>urac</code> in a service's config and it gets all of them, with no auth code in the service.
				</p>
				<p><a href="/docs/concepts/gateway/">Read the gateway reference →</a></p>
			</div>
			<ol class="pipeline">
				<li class="edge"><div><strong>Protect the edge</strong><span>IP ban list and global maintenance mode.</span></div></li>
				<li><div><strong>Resolve the tenant</strong><span>The external key identifies tenant, application and product package.</span></div></li>
				<li><div><strong>Check the package ACL</strong><span>Is this service, version and API in the package the key belongs to?</span></div></li>
				<li><div><strong>Validate the token</strong><span>OAuth access token, including tenant-restricted and device-bound tokens.</span></div></li>
				<li><div><strong>Apply user and tenant access</strong><span>URAC user, group and per-tenant overrides.</span></div></li>
				<li><div><strong>Shape traffic</strong><span>Idempotency-Key replay protection, rate limiting and GET response caching.</span></div></li>
				<li class="edge"><div><strong>Route the call</strong><span>Found through the service registry and forwarded with tenant context attached.</span></div></li>
			</ol>
		</div>
	</section>



	<section id="gateway-features">
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="section-head">
				<p class="eyebrow">The gateway, in full</p>
				<h2>Everything the gateway does for every service behind it</h2>
				<p class="lead">All of it is configuration: in the registry, the console or a service's config file. None of it is code in your services.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="grid grid-3 feature-cards">
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Tenants &amp; keys</h3>
					<ul>
						<li>Tenant, application and product package resolved from each external key</li>
						<li>Key expiry dates</li>
						<li>Per-key IP allow and deny lists</li>
						<li>Per-key device rules by browser and user agent</li>
						<li>Per-tenant service configuration and tenant profiles</li>
						<li>Package overrides per network</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Access control</h3>
					<ul>
						<li>Package ACL per environment, service, version, HTTP method and API</li>
						<li>Public, signed-in or group-only APIs</li>
						<li>Restricted mode: only listed APIs are reachable</li>
						<li>Application-level ACL overrides</li>
						<li>User groups that grant their own packages</li>
						<li>Memberships resolved per product</li>
						<li><code>/soajs/acl</code> so sockets and other servers share the same rules</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Identity &amp; tokens</h3>
					<ul>
						<li>OAuth 2.0 access tokens checked on every private API</li>
						<li>The signed-in user and their groups forwarded to your service</li>
						<li>Tenant-restricted tokens for cross-tenant pairing</li>
						<li>Device-bound tokens checked against a <code>device-id</code> header</li>
						<li>Login through the oauth service: password, LDAP, OpenAM, social, Azure AD, PIN or phone code</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Traffic</h3>
					<ul>
						<li>Rate limiting by tenant or by tenant and IP</li>
						<li>Separate strategies for public and private APIs, overridable per service</li>
						<li>IP ban list</li>
						<li>GET response caching with per-API TTL, in memory or MongoDB</li>
						<li>Idempotency-Key replay protection for writes</li>
						<li>CORS settings per environment</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Routing &amp; operations</h3>
					<ul>
						<li>Service registry and discovery, routing by service name and version</li>
						<li>Tenant and user context forwarded on every call</li>
						<li>Proxy calls to another environment's gateway with its own key</li>
						<li>Roaming between deployments, carrying the verified tenant and user</li>
						<li>Global and per-service maintenance mode, readiness checks and registry reload</li>
						<li>Last-seen tracking with triggers by service, method or API</li>
						<li>Error logs with sensitive values redacted</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Runs where you do</h3>
					<ul>
						<li>Node.js gateway on Express 5, scaled out with replicas</li>
						<li>Go gateway in preview: smaller image, faster start</li>
						<li>Kubernetes with Helm, cloud or on-prem</li>
						<li>Fully air-gapped deployments</li>
						<li>Services in Node.js, Go or Python behind the same rules</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p style="margin-top:24px"><a href="/docs/concepts/gateway/">Gateway reference →</a></p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="soft" id="how-it-fits">
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="section-head">
				<p class="eyebrow">How it fits together</p>
				<h2>A multi-tenant, real-time product on SOAJS</h2>
				<p class="lead">Apps, a real-time server, background jobs and your own services all go through one gateway. Tenancy, ACL and tokens are decided there, once.</p>
			</div>
			<ArchitectureDiagram />
		</div>
	</section>
	<section>
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="section-head">
				<p class="eyebrow">Beyond the gateway</p>
				<h2>The rest of the platform</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="grid grid-2">
				<div class="card">
					<h3><a href="/platform/#imfv">Input validation</a></h3>
					<p class="muted">Declare each API's inputs, sources and JSON-schema validation in config. Requests arrive at your handler already mapped and validated.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3><a href="/platform/#identity">Users, groups and login</a></h3>
					<p class="muted">The urac and oauth services manage users, groups, invitations and every login method, per tenant.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3><a href="/platform/#console">Console and marketplace</a></h3>
					<p class="muted">A web console for environments, tenants, keys, products, packages, the service catalog, Git repositories and infrastructure.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3><a href="/platform/#deploy">Deploy and SDKs</a></h3>
					<p class="muted">Helm charts for every core service, in-container deployment from source, and SDKs for Node.js, Go and Python.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="soft">
		<div class="wrap grid grid-2" style="align-items:center">
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow">Configuration, not boilerplate</p>
				<h2>Three flags instead of an auth layer</h2>
				<p class="lead">
					A service's config tells the gateway who may call it, which APIs it exposes, and how each input is
					sourced and validated. By the time your handler runs, the caller is known and the input in
					<code>req.soajs.inputmaskData</code> is already clean.
				</p>
				<p><a href="/docs/getting-started/first-service/">Build your first service →</a></p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="code-label">config.js</div>
				<Code code={serviceConfig} lang="js" theme="github-dark" class="code" />
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="section-head">
				<p class="eyebrow">Built on SOAJS</p>
				<h2>We build our own products on it</h2>
				<p class="lead">Encrypted communications, regulated healthcare, IoT, fintech and social: our team runs each of these on SOAJS. Many more products have been built on it by other teams.</p>
			</div>
			<dl class="stats" style="margin-bottom:40px">
				<div><dt>{totals.services}</dt><dd>microservices across our products</dd></div>
				<div><dt>{totals.platforms}</dt><dd>multi-tenant products</dd></div>
				<div><dt>23</dt><dd>services behind a single gateway in Connect Spaces</dd></div>
			</dl>
			<div class="grid grid-3">
				{platforms.map((p) => (
					<div class="card">
						<h3><a href={`/built-on-soajs/#${p.id}`}>{p.name}</a></h3>
						<p class="muted" style="margin-bottom:8px">{p.domain}</p>
						<p>{p.summary}</p>
					</div>
				))}
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="band">
				<div>
					<h2>Try it on your own cluster</h2>
					<p>Install the gateway and core services with Helm, then put your first service behind it.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="actions">
					<a class="btn btn-primary" href="/docs/getting-started/quickstart/">Quickstart</a>
					<a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://github.com/soajs/soajs">Star on GitHub</a>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
